 Problem Statement
A website needs a clear and easy way for users to move from one page to another. Without proper
navigation, users may find it difficult to locate important pages. The CSS-Based Navigation System will
create a simple, attractive, and user-friendly navigation menu using HTML and CSS.
2. Project Objectives
• Create a simple navigation bar.
• Help users easily move between web pages.
• Use CSS to improve the design and appearance.
• Add hover effects to links.
• Highlight the active page.
• Make the navigation easy to understand and use.
• Make the menu responsive for different screen sizes.
3. Functional Requirements
• Display navigation links.
• Allow users to click links and open different pages.
• Provide hover effects when the mouse moves over a link.
• Show which page is currently active.
• Provide horizontal or vertical navigation.
• Work correctly on common web browsers.
• Provide a responsive menu for mobile devices.
4. Non-Functional Requirements
• Easy to use: Users should understand the menu quickly.
• Fast: Navigation should load quickly.
• Attractive: The design should look clean and professional.
• Responsive: It should work on computers, tablets, and mobile phones.
• Reliable: Links should work correctly.
• Maintainable: CSS should be organized so changes are easy to make.
5. Feasibility Study
A. Technical Feasibility
The project is technically possible because it requires basic web technologies: HTML, CSS, a web
browser, and a text/code editor. No expensive or advanced software is required.
B. Economic Feasibility
The project has low cost because HTML and CSS are free, free code editors can be used, no special
hardware is required, and development and maintenance costs are low.
C. Operational Feasibility
The system is easy to operate because users only need to click navigation links. The interface is simple,
requires little or no training, and improves the website experience.
6. Project Scope
The project will focus on creating a CSS-based navigation menu with navigation links, hover effects,
active-link styling, a simple layout, and responsive design.
7. Expected Outcome
The final system will provide a simple, attractive, responsive, and user-friendly navigation bar that allows
users to move easily between different pages of a website.
